+# Analysis Report
+
+## Summary
+| Metric | Value |
+|--------|-------|
+| Approaches evaluated | 3 |
+| Top recommendation | Approach B — Cursor-Based Incremental Poll |
+| Confidence | High |
+| Divergent assessments | 1 |
+
+## Context Weights
+| Dimension | Weight | Rationale |
+|-----------|--------|-----------|
+| Technical Soundness | 20% | Single read-only endpoint; architecture is straightforward — less differentiating |
+| Future Flexibility | 15% | Patterns set precedent for STORY-010/012/013/017 but not the primary selection driver |
+| Business Value | 25% | Bucket-1 priority; immediate data-warehouse value; no Amazon analog today |
+| Implementation Effort | 25% | Week 3 of 4-week staging window; competing with STORY-009 cert critical path |
+| Risk Profile | 15% | Read-only ingest with low blast radius; risk matters but less than value + effort |
+
+## Evaluation Agents
+| Agent | Dimensions | Approaches Scored |
+|-------|-----------|------------------|
+| Technical | Soundness + Flexibility | 3 |
+| Business | Value + Effort | 3 |
+| Risk | Risk Profile + Register | 3 |
+
+## Scoring Matrix
+| Approach | Technical (20%) | Flexibility (15%) | Value (25%) | Effort (25%) | Risk (15%) | Weighted |
+|----------|-----------|------------|-------|--------|------|----------|
+| A — Simple Periodic Poll | 4/5 | 2/5 | 3/5 | 5/5 | 4/5 | 3.70 |
+| B — Cursor-Based Incremental Poll | 4/5 | 4/5 | 5/5 | 3/5 | 3/5 | 3.85 |
+| C — Batch Window Poll | 3/5 | 3/5 | 4/5 | 2/5 | 2/5 | 2.85 |
+
+## Divergent Assessments
+- **Approach A**: Risk evaluator scores it highest (4/5 — fewest failure modes, fits timeline), while Business evaluator scores it lowest on value (3/5 — sets poor patterns for the greenfield repo, no enum validation). Both perspectives have merit because A is genuinely the safest path to "something works" but leaves data-quality and pattern-reuse debt that compounds across STORY-010/012/013.
+
+## Top 3 Ranking
+
+### 1. Approach B — Cursor-Based Incremental Poll (Weighted: 3.85)
+- **Why #1:** Best balance of business value and technical quality. Incremental polling reduces redundant API calls, enum validation table directly addresses OQ-9, and 90-day retention keeps operational queries fast. Sets the strongest reusable pattern for downstream ingest stories.
+- **Key strength:** Highest business value (5/5) — complete data coverage with proper enum handling and retention strategy that downstream consumers (Finance, cross-channel analytics) can depend on.
+- **Key risk:** R-B-2 — client-side cursor filtering relies on undocumented `last_modified` field ordering; must validate during STORY-007 staging discovery. If ordering assumption fails, degrade gracefully to Approach A behavior with dedup.
+- **Trade-off:** 2-day build vs. 1-day for Approach A; moderate complexity adds ~150 LOC. Acceptable given the pattern-setting value for the greenfield repo.
+
+### 2. Approach A — Simple Periodic Poll (Weighted: 3.70)
+- **Why #2:** Fastest to deliver and lowest operational risk. If Week 3 timeline becomes critically constrained by STORY-009 certification, this is the fallback.
+- **Key strength:** Highest effort score (5/5) — ~200 LOC, ~1 day to build, fewest moving parts.
+- **Key risk:** R-A-2/R-A-3 — indefinite retention causes unbounded table growth; FK to orders table may fail if STORY-009 is not yet complete.
+- **Trade-off:** Speed over quality. No cursor, no retention strategy, no enum validation — each becomes tech debt that must be addressed before production.
+
+### 3. Approach C — Batch Window Poll (Weighted: 2.85)
+- **Why #3:** Production-grade infrastructure (partitioning, enum lookup FK, batch processing) is disproportionate to a single non-paginated endpoint with unknown data volume.
+- **Key strength:** Best long-term operational model if return volumes grow to millions of records.
+- **Key risk:** R-C-3 — 500 LOC and 3-day estimate exceeds Week 3 time budget with STORY-009 certification on the critical path. R-C-2 — enum lookup FK directly conflicts with OQ-9 being unresolved.
+- **Trade-off:** Operational maturity at the cost of timeline risk and premature infrastructure.
+
+## Risk Register Highlights (Top 3 approaches)
+| ID | Approach | Risk | Severity | Likelihood | Mitigation |
+|----|----------|------|----------|------------|-----------|
+| R-B-2 | B | Client-side cursor filtering relies on undocumented field ordering | High | Med | Validate during STORY-007 staging discovery; degrade to full-fetch + dedup if unordered |
+| R-B-1 | B | Cursor state loss causes silent data gap | Med | Med | Store cursor in `poll_state` table; fallback to full fetch on loss; dedup absorbs duplicates |
+| R-B-3 | B | 90-day archive adds operational complexity | Med | Med | Defer archive to follow-up story; ship with `is_archived` flag only in v1 |
+| R-A-1 | A | Unbounded response growth — full fetch may OOM as returns accumulate | High | Med | Monitor response size in staging; circuit breaker at 10 MB |
+| R-A-3 | A | FK to STORY-009 orders table blocks inserts if orders not yet ingested | High | High | Use nullable soft FK or DEFERRABLE constraint; reconciliation job |
+| R-A-2 | A | Indefinite retention bloats Postgres without bound | Med | High | Config-driven retention cutoff; document as tech-debt |
+| R-C-1 | C | Partitioned tables premature for unknown data volume | High | High | Do not partition until data volume justifies it |
+| R-C-2 | C | Enum lookup FK conflicts with unresolved OQ-9 | High | High | Use VARCHAR + UPSERT-on-first-seen; never hard-code enum in migration |
+| R-C-3 | C | 500 LOC exceeds Week 3 time budget alongside STORY-009 cert | High | High | Cut scope to converge toward Approach B; or defer to Week 4 |
+
+## Cross-Cutting Concerns
+
+**Shared patterns at risk of inconsistent implementation:**
+STORY-010 (listing errors), STORY-012 (finance), STORY-013 (pricing), and STORY-017 (async reports) all need identical patterns: poll loop, upsert dedup, unknown-enum handling, DI'd client with `respx` mock, and soft FK to upstream tables. If STORY-011 ships these ad-hoc, other stories will copy inconsistent implementations.
+
+**Mitigation:** Extract shared `app/common/poll.py` (poll loop + dedup + state tracking) and `app/common/enum_handling.py` (tag-and-continue for unknown values) during STORY-011 Phase 8. Adds ~50 LOC to STORY-011 but saves 200+ LOC of duplicated logic across the epic. Add to Phase 7 defensive test gate.
+
+**Epic Migration Risk:**
+Stories 009, 010, 011, 012, 013, and 017 each create an Alembic migration in parallel worktrees. If any two merge without rebasing, Alembic detects multiple heads and `alembic upgrade head` fails. Week 3 (STORY-009 + 011 + 013 landing simultaneously) is highest-risk window.
+
+**Mitigation:** Every story branch runs `alembic heads` before PR; if multiple heads, create merge migration (`alembic merge heads -m "merge STORY-0XX"`). CI gates on `alembic heads | wc -l` equaling 1.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Select Approach B — Cursor-Based Incremental Poll** with the following scope adjustments for v1:
+
+1. **Defer archive table to follow-up** — ship with `is_archived` boolean flag but no partition moves or retention cron in v1. Keeps complexity at ~300 LOC.
+2. **Validate cursor assumption in STORY-007** — if `last_modified` ordering is unreliable, degrade to Approach A's full-fetch + dedup (the dedup logic is identical; only the fetch strategy changes).
+3. **Extract shared poll/enum patterns** — `app/common/poll.py` and `app/common/enum_handling.py` during Phase 8 to set the standard for STORY-010/012/013/017.
+4. **Soft FK to orders** — nullable `order_id` column with no DB-level FK constraint in v1; reconciliation check as a separate test (`test_order_linkage.py`).
+
+This gives us the highest business value within the Week 3 timeline, sets clean patterns for the remaining 5 ingest stories, and degrades gracefully if staging discovery (STORY-007) invalidates the cursor assumption.
+
+## Phase 4 Deliverables — Polling Cadence, Dedup, Retention
+
+Per seed.md required outputs for Phase 4:
+
+| Decision | Recommendation | Rationale |
+|----------|---------------|-----------|
+| **Polling cadence** | 10 minutes, configurable via env var `RETURNS_POLL_INTERVAL_SEC=600` | Balances freshness vs. API courtesy; returns are not time-critical. Configurable for staging (shorter) vs. production (longer). |
+| **Dedup key** | `ProductReturn.id` (Mongo ObjectId) as Postgres PK | Verified unique per the OpenAPI spec; ObjectId format confirmed. `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE` for upsert. |
+| **Retention strategy** | Indefinite in v1 with `is_archived` flag; 90-day active window for queries via filtered index | Defer physical archival to follow-up story. Filtered index `WHERE NOT is_archived` keeps operational queries fast. |
+| **Unknown enum handling** | Store raw value in `return_reason` (VARCHAR); tag with `return_reason_known = false` if not in validation set | Tag-and-continue pattern; never crash on unknown. Validation set populated by STORY-007. |
+
+## Follow-ups
+- Validate `last_modified` ordering assumption during STORY-007 staging discovery
+- Refund itemization (OQ-21) — if data appears in payload, escalate to PM for schema design discussion
+- Physical archive/retention cron — separate story after production data volume is known
+- Shared `app/common/poll.py` extraction scope to be confirmed in Phase 6 feature spec
